On Wednesday, March 4, 2015 at 6:34:47 PM UTC+1, Dan S wrote:
> Hi -
>
> Idea 2 is called the "semi-Markov model". I hope that helps your
> literature search :)
> Egotistically enough, I'll point you to a short note I wrote recently
> about what to call them:
> http://rspb.royalsocietypublishing.org/content/281/1792/20141370.e-letters
>
> In response to the question about choosing between the MM and SMM, I'd
> say choose whichever fits closest to your beliefs about the underlying
> behaviour. Note that a non-hidden Markov model will always generate
> "holding times" of geometrically-distributed length. If a priori you
> don't think that fits well to your situation, then that's a good
> argument to use your SMM.
>
> Best
> Dan
>
> 2015-03-04 17:24 GMT+00:00  <matus.s...@privatdemail.net>:
> > I have a Markov chain model with four states A, B, C and D. The transition
> > probabilities are given by the 4x4 transition matrix M. The data are given
> > as chains of states e.g. AAABBBACCCAA .... How would you parametrize this
> > model?  How would you parametrize a hierarchical extension of the model? For
> > hierarchical extension assume that I have N subjects and for each subject I
> > have M_n chains.
> >
> > Idea 1: Estimate transition probabilities directly. I would then use inverse
> > logit to transform the transition probabilities and pool the transformed
> > vars with a hierarchical gaussian prior.
> >
> > Idea 2: In the actual data, the states repeat very often. Same-state chains
> > of length 100 and more are not unusual. As a consequence the values on the
> > diagonal of the transition matrix will be near 1 and other elements will be
> > near 0. My alternative idea is to estimate the transition probabilities
> > between different consecutive states. I would estimate the length of a chain
> > of same-state transitions with a poisson model. Again, there is a multilevel
> > generalization of poisson model in chap. 15 of gelman's DAUR&MHM. I would
> > adapt this.
> >
> > I haven't coded anything yet. I'm just interested in your opinion. Which
> > idea is better or do you see alternative formulations?
> >
> > My hunch is that idea 2 is easier to estimate with STAN and also the
> > parameters are easier to interpret. However, I've never seen idea 2 in the
> > literature, so I'm not very confident.
