From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Thu Mar  5 13:59:18 2015
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Thu, 05 Mar 2015 13:59:18 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] How would you parametrize a markov chain model and its hierarchical extension?
Message-ID: <pIEJOTEtbAU.0rylVFX3HeQJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <pIEJOTEtbAU.w45LWMLKiloJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <pIEJOTEtbAU.KZkK0r9qh3IJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <pIEJOTEtbAU.w45LWMLKiloJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/pIEJOTEtbAU/m/0rylVFX3HeQJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The simplest thing to do would be what Luc suggested --- use a Dirichlet
prior and give it some kind of hyperprior (typically reparameterizing
in terms of a simplex mean alpha/sum(alpha) and a prior on the total
count sum(alpha).  Then each state would draw its transition probabilities
from Dirichlet(alpha).

You could enrich this using a logistic multinormal.  Just use
a multivariate normal, then softmax transform the draws to get
your simplexes for the transitions.  This was one of the first
pieces of stats advice Andrew ever gave me, long before we
started working together.

- Bob

