Date: Mon, 09 Mar 2015 07:22:17 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] fitting multiple sets of data with same model
Message-ID: <dP3mYsoBycc.UzPEIn4ywzIJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <dP3mYsoBycc.m0PaBAoXGZgJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <dP3mYsoBycc.m0PaBAoXGZgJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/dP3mYsoBycc/m/UzPEIn4ywzIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'm going to try to clarify the question first.  Suppose
your data looks like this:

  vector[N] y[K];
  vector[N] x[K];

and parameters you want to estimate are:

  real beta[K];
  real sigma[K];

You should get the same posterior for each beta[k]
and sigma[k] if you have a single Stan program with
likelihood:

  for (k in 1:K) {
    y[k] ~ normal(beta[k] * x[k], sigma[k]);
    beta[k] ~ ... some prior ...
    sigma[k] ~ .. some prior ...
  }

or if you run K separate instances of Stan with
likelihood

  y[k] ~ normal(beta[k] * x[k], sigma[k];
  beta[k] ~ ... some prior ...
  sigma[k] ~ .. some prior ...

Now if you provide a hierarchical prior things will be
different, which I'd strongly advise if the K instances
are exchangeable.

- Bob

