On Wednesday, March 11, 2015 at 11:10:44 AM UTC-4, Cameron Bracken wrote:
>
> I am fitting hundreds of GEV distributions in spatial setting so all the GEV parameters (location, scale and shape) are vectors. The constraint is that 1 + (y - loc) * shape / scale > 0, for all y. Setting these constraints in a univariate case is strait forward, but every element of the parameter vectors needs a different constraint.

Does the following work for you? The idea is to decompose the scale parameter into a unit_scale parameter and a multiple thereof. Given y, loc, shape and unit_scale, you can figure out what multiple has to be such that (y - loc) * shape / (multiple * unit_scale) > -1 for all observations.

parameters {
  vector[N] loc;
  vector[N] shape;
  vector<lower=0>[N] unit_scale;
  real<lower=abs(min(0,min((y - loc) * shape ./ unit_scale)))> multiple;
}
transformed parameters {
  vector<lower=0>[N] scale;
  scale <- unit_scale * multiple;
}

Ben

From uda33143a8db60ce8 at groups.google.com  Wed Mar 11 20:44:56 2015
From: uda33143a8db60ce8 at groups.google.com (Andrew Gelman)
Date: Wed, 11 Mar 2015 20:44:56 +0000
Subject: Bayesian identification using ordered priors
Message-ID: <pt34sBaCHk8.btu04t8GC-kJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <pt34sBaCHk8.1doKcSo1kWIJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <pt34sBaCHk8.H5nJMYyapMcJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <pt34sBaCHk8.1doKcSo1kWIJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/pt34sBaCHk8/m/btu04t8GC-kJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> On Mar 11, 2015, at 2:13 PM, Thomas <daub...@tcd.ie> wrote:
>
> I tried the ordered vector suggestion for the reflection invariance, but that doesn’t work since the two constrained ideal points may end up being placed very close together (that's similar to what happens when using two half-normals).

The ordering should work even if the two points are close.  But if that is a concern (that you don’t want to constrain them to be ordered), you can simply give them different priors with equal variances but with one prior having a mean lower than the other.  This should break the aliasing and identify the model.  In general we much prefer this sort of identification strategy, (rather than having an unidentified model and then summing to average over aliases).

cc-ing Bob because he likes to collect this sort of advice

