Subject: Re: [stan-users] How to obtain a submatrix
Message-ID: <DpFGIZiJOJw.5Qx36czTBV4J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <DpFGIZiJOJw.hJ_NP3QY8eQJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <DpFGIZiJOJw.hJ_NP3QY8eQJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/DpFGIZiJOJw/m/5Qx36czTBV4J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

There's no built-in way to do that.  You have to write
the loops yourself.  If you want to get rid of row and
column n in an N x N matrix, then you can do it one
quadrant at a time, as so:

matrix delete_row_col(matrix a, int n) {
  matrix[rows(a) - 1, cols(a) - 1] result;
  for (i in 1:(n-1)) {
    for (j in 1:(n-1))
      result[i,j] <- a[i,j];
    for (j in (n+1):N)
      result[i,j-1] <- a[i,j];
  }
  for (i in (n+1):N) {
    for (j in 1:(n-1))
      result[i-1,j] <- a[i,j];
    for (j in (n+1):N)
      result[i-1,j-1] <- a[i,j];
  }
  return result;
}

Of course, if you don't know the row/col, then you need to write
another loop to find that first.  And if you have more than one match,
then the function gets trickier, but you could reduce it to removing
one at a time.

- Bob

