>
>     = log_sum_exp_{k in 1:K} (log Pr[n in k | ...] + log Pr[m in k | ...] );
>

Sometimes I forget the basics, especially when working in these higher-level frameworks. This is very close to what we are trying to infer. However, If we calculated the probability of two proteins coming from the same complex, how would we combine this information to assign multiple memberships per protein? Re-reading the formulation I gave in the prior email, I think I oversimplified it a bit. The co-migration is not restricted to two proteins, it can be anywhere between 2-50 proteins. So I guess the probability of interest is something in the line of,

  Pr[n in same cluster as n’, n’’, … | y]

can this be calculated downstream from the Pr[n in same cluster as n’| y]? Calculating it for all given combinations seems unfeasible.

Digging deeper in the google scholar pages, I actually stumped on this article http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~sguennem/publications/ICDM2013a.pdf. It describes a mixed membership mixture model (I am assuming it is a legal copy, since it comes from the main authors web site.) Of course, this does not capture the generative process exactly, as each of the places are physically connected sequentially (i.e. 1-2-3-4-5, …). But practically, that might be OK. In theory, I should be able to implement the model in Stan using the same summarisation as for a normal mixture model, right?

> Then I'd probably try to add some multivariate structure to the clustering,
> but first things first.
>
> And I don't think you'll need 1000 clusters --- how many clusters do you think
> are actually operating?

If each of the clusters represent a physical complex (a set of proteins), and assuming each protein can be a member of multiple clusters, 1,000 is not an unreasonable high number.

>
> The nice part about this is that the Pr[n and m in same cluster | ...] calcs
> are not sensitive to label switching.

David

