Date: Thu, 19 Mar 2015 15:00:22 +0000
Subject: Performance penalty using truncated priors?
Message-ID: <-9wCHUkjTr8.U3qKXygQD38J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/-9wCHUkjTr8/m/U3qKXygQD38J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all

I am working with additive models where I frequently need to constrain the sign of the fitting coefficient, e.g.

y = a * x1 + b * x2 + c * x3

say I want a to be positive and have a decreasing prior for large values. I typically do this using one of these priors

a ~ normal(0, 50) T[0.0,];

or

a ~ gamma(1, 0.025);

where I define

real<lower=0> a;

in both cases.

My question is whether I suffer in performance, using the truncated normal distribution compared to the inherently positive gamma distribution?

Thanks in advance,
Per

From ucab04b8f0759c712 at groups.google.com  Thu Mar 19 15:13:22 2015
From: ucab04b8f0759c712 at groups.google.com (Daniel Lee)
Date: Thu, 19 Mar 2015 15:13:22 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Performance penalty using truncated priors?
Message-ID: <-9wCHUkjTr8.Oe3X2uBXA_0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <-9wCHUkjTr8.U3qKXygQD38J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <-9wCHUkjTr8.U3qKXygQD38J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/-9wCHUkjTr8/m/Oe3X2uBXA_0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Per,

I'll start by answering your question:
  No, you will not suffer in computational performance by using the
truncated normal distribution, or any other distribution.

If you declare `a` as a parameter:
   real<lower=0> a;

Truncating the prior is computationally worse. Instead of

   a ~ normal(0, 50) T[0.0,];

use
   a ~ normal(0, 50);

The additive model might really suffer with such wide priors if the
data don't provide much information.

Question: have you tried both the normal and gamma? The nice thing
about Stan is that you can actually build both models and try them out
on your data.

Daniel

