Subject: Re: [stan-users] Performance penalty using truncated priors?
Message-ID: <-9wCHUkjTr8.bko6MDmgaRAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <-9wCHUkjTr8.Oe3X2uBXA_0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <-9wCHUkjTr8.U3qKXygQD38J@stan-users.groups.google.com> <-9wCHUkjTr8.Oe3X2uBXA_0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/-9wCHUkjTr8/m/bko6MDmgaRAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

We tend to prefer something that focuses the mass
closer to the expected values, but has wide tails, like
a Cauchy.  There are now sections in the regression chapter
of the manual with a discussion of recommended priors.

- Bob

> On Mar 20, 2015, at 2:13 AM, Daniel Lee <bea...@alum.mit.edu> wrote:
>
> Hi Per,
>
> I'll start by answering your question:
>  No, you will not suffer in computational performance by using the
> truncated normal distribution, or any other distribution.
>
> If you declare `a` as a parameter:
>   real<lower=0> a;
>
> Truncating the prior is computationally worse. Instead of
>   a ~ normal(0, 50) T[0.0,];
> use
>   a ~ normal(0, 50);
>
> The additive model might really suffer with such wide priors if the
> data don't provide much information.
>
>
> Question: have you tried both the normal and gamma? The nice thing
> about Stan is that you can actually build both models and try them out
> on your data.
>
>
> Daniel
>
>
>
> On Thu, Mar 19, 2015 at 11:00 AM, Per Nielsen <evi...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> Hi all
>>
>> I am working with additive models where I frequently need to constrain the
>> sign of the fitting coefficient, e.g.
>>
>> y = a * x1 + b * x2 + c * x3
>>
>> say I want a to be positive and have a decreasing prior for large values. I
>> typically do this using one of these priors
>>
>> a ~ normal(0, 50) T[0.0,];
>>
>> or
>>
>> a ~ gamma(1, 0.025);
>>
>> where I define
>>
>> real<lower=0> a;
>>
>> in both cases.
>>
>> My question is whether I suffer in performance, using the truncated normal
>> distribution compared to the inherently positive gamma distribution?
>>
>> Thanks in advance,
>> Per
>>
