(I am happy to be included in reference materials, etc., no restrictions)

Hello Stan Users,

I am also trying to estimate a growth mixture model in Stan, but I am not sure if I am doing it correctly. I have read the mixture model chapter from the manual. If I am doing it correctly, than I am almost surely doing it poorly. So far the model compiles and begins sampling, but on my machine it has taken about 6 hours to get to 10% of 2000 iterations [Warmup] for Chain 1. In a previous attempt to let the model run overnight, just a single chain, 200 iterations, the effective sample size for k(# of components) was very small relative to the other parameters, but I don't remember the specifics before my computer froze.

The model I am trying to estimate is just as Robert described. In this case the repeated measures are described as a function of three potentially correlated latent variables, vector "eta" ("eta[,1]"= intercept, "eta[,2]"= slope, "eta[,3]"= quadratic). There is only a single estimated error variance "sigma_y", so the residual variance is equal at each time point. The only parameters that vary by class are the means on the latent variables "mu_eta". The parameters of interest are the class-specific mean vectors for eta, the covariance matrix among the etas ("Psi"), the probabilities of belonging to each class "pi", and the residual error variance.

I am attaching example fake data for a two-class quadratic growth mixture model, with 5 repeated observations "T" for 2000 individuals "N" (the data has 6 columns, the last column is the true latent class assignment). I was able to get this model to work as a latent growth curve model in the log-scale (without bringing in the classes), but in translating to a mixture model it seems to be going awry.

Any feedback is appreciated. Thank you.

My code setup (in R):
y<-read.table('gmmq1.dat')
y<-y[,1:5]
x<- c(0, 1, 2, 3, 4)
q<- c(0, 1, 4, 9, 16)
Omega <- structure(c(5, 0, 0, 0, 5, 0, 0, 0, 5), .Dim = c(3, 3))
gmm_dat<- list(N = nrow(y), T = ncol(y), x = x, q = q, y = y, Omega=Omega, K=2)
gmm_fit <- stan(file = 'gmm-q.stan', data = gmm_dat, verbose = FALSE)

Stan code:
data {
  int<lower=0>  N;// number of ppl
  int<lower=0>  T;// number of timepoints
  real x[T];      // X (time)
  real q[T];      // q (time^2)
  real y[N,T];    // Y matrix of T obs of N ppl
  int <lower=1> K;// number of classes
  cov_matrix[3] Omega;
}
transformed data {
}
parameters {
  simplex[K] pi;
  vector[3] eta[N];  // eta (int, slope, quad) for each person
  vector[3] mu_eta[K];  // mean (int, slope, quad) for etas
  cov_matrix[3] Psi; // cov matrix among etas
  real<lower=0> sigmasq_y; // y residual error variance
}
transformed parameters {
  real<lower=0> sigma_y;
  vector[K] log_q_z[N];
  sigma_y <- sqrt(sigmasq_y);
  for (n in 1:N){
    log_q_z[n]<-log(pi);
    for (t in 1:T)
      for(k in 1:K)
         log_q_z[n,k]<-log_q_z[n,k]+normal_log(y[n,t], eta[n, 1] +eta[n, 2]*x[t] +eta[n,3]*q[t], sigma_y);
  }
}
model {
   real tt[K];
   mu_eta[K] ~ normal(0, 100);
   sigmasq_y ~ inv_gamma(0.001, 0.001); //y residual error variance drawn from prior
   Psi~ inv_wishart(3, Omega); //cov matrix among etas drawn from prior
   for (n in 1:N)
     for (k in 1:K)
      tt[k]<-log(pi[k])+multi_normal_log(eta[n],mu_eta[k],Psi);

   for (n in 1:N){
       increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(log_q_z[n]));
       increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(tt));
  }
}

