Hi everyone!

I'm having a hard time trying to implement a HMM in Stan. I'm not sure I understood what the reference guide means when it says that the latent states have to be marginalized out.
Let us say my likelihood for a single unit is
 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-ze1uCsQOR6k/VRVB5cdZfiI/AAAAAAAAAP8/bwInCCiTchI/s1600/Likelihood.PNG>

Here I marginalized out over all the possible latent states S in order to express the likelihood as a function of the conditional probability of the observed variable Y with respect to the latent state S, and the transition probabilities between the hidden states. To be more precise, I obtain
 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-glG4BwvLJIo/VRVC-YkTIRI/AAAAAAAAAQE/Z4w1gzHi1Xg/s1600/Likelihood2.PNG>
When I try to implement this simple model in STAN, as below

data {
  int<lower=1> n;
  int<lower=1> n_tot;
  int<lower=1> k;
  int<lower=1> s;                 // number of states of the HMM
  int<lower=1> T[n];
  int<lower=0> T_in[n];
  matrix<lower=0>[n_tot,k] X;
  matrix<lower=0>[n_tot,k+1] Z;
  int<lower=0> N[n_tot];
  int<lower=0,upper=1> C[n_tot];
  vector[s] alpha_in;
}

parameters {
  vector[s] eta;
  matrix[k,s] tau;
  vector[s] alpha;
  matrix[k+1,s] gamma;
  simplex[s] trans[s];
}

transformed parameters {
  matrix<lower=0>[n_tot,s] lambda;
  matrix<lower=0,upper=1>[n_tot,s] m;

  {int T_act;
  for (i in 1:n){
    for (t in 1:T[i]){
      T_act <- T_in[i]+t;
      lambda[T_act] <- exp(eta' + X[T_act] * tau);
      for (j in 1:s){
        m[T_act,j] <- inv_logit(alpha[j] + (Z[T_act] * col(gamma,j)));
      }
    }
  }
  }
}

model {
  // Priors
  eta ~ normal(0,100);
  alpha ~ normal(0,100);

  for (i in 1:5){
    tau[i] ~ normal(0,100);
  }
  for (i in 1:6){
    gamma[i] ~ normal(0,100);
  }

  for (i in 1:s){
    trans[i] ~ dirichlet(alpha_in);
  }

  {int T_act; int stat[n_tot];

  // Likelihood
  for (i in 1:n){
    T_act <- T_in[i]+1;
    stat[T_act] <- 1;
    N[T_act] ~ poisson(lambda[T_act,stat[T_act]]);
    C[T_act] ~ bernoulli(m[T_act,stat[T_act]]);

    for (t in 2:T[i]){
      T_act <- T_in[i]+t;
      stat[T_act] ~ categorical(trans[stat[T_act-1]]);
      N[T_act] ~ poisson(lambda[T_act,stat[T_act]]);
      C[T_act] ~ bernoulli(m[T_act,stat[T_act]]);
    }
  }
  }
}

I always get the following error:

The current Metropolis proposal is about to be rejected because of the following issue:
stan::prob::categorical_log(i): Number of categories is 0, but must be between (1, 3)

I think I am doing a conceptual mistake: the local data need to be set explicitly before being used, but I do not know the hidden states!

My question is: how can I sample the hidden states from the transition matrix (which is a parameter to be inferred)? Is it compulsory to use an algorithm (e.g. the forward algorithm) as indicated in STAN user's guide ?

Thank you all for any help

